{
  "dom_id": "step-q277916",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "In **ascending order**, the roots of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$\nand\n$\\,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+4)(x-2)}{(x-3)(x-6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$, in ascending order, are $x=-4$ and $x=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
